Enumerate input devices one at a time so card aliases stay visible

Follow-up to the capture self-block fix, found by walking the UMC1820
field configuration through the fixed code: get_input_device collected
every enumerated device into a Vec before matching. cpal opens each
device for capture during enumeration and keeps the handle for the
device's lifetime, so on a card with a single capture substream the first
alias enumerated (hw:CARD=X) held the substream while the rest of the
collection ran - every other alias of the same card (plughw:, dsnoop:,
sysdefault:) failed its capture open and silently dropped out of the
device list. The recommended plughw: name for such a card could never
resolve, and the collected list could also disagree with --list-inputs
numbering (which iterates lazily), breaking selection by index.

get_input_device now makes two lazy passes: the first collects names
only, dropping each device before the next opens, so every alias
enumerates and the numbering matches --list-inputs exactly; the choice of
device (exact name, then index, then ALSA card match) is a pure function
over those names; the second pass fetches only the chosen device by name.
A device that vanishes between passes falls through to the existing
not-found report with the enumerated names and ALSA probe.

Verification: the resolver decision order is unit-tested (exact name
beats index, index bounds, ALSA card fallback, unmatched); tests were
written first and failed before the implementation. Clean release build
with zero warnings; full suite green (507 lib + 28 http-api + 1
broker-restart + 26 streaming). The alias-blocking behavior itself needs
a real single-substream capture card, so field confirmation comes from
the UMC1820 install.

